## 10. PROVENANCE / OBS / SEC EVIDENCE

Provenance evidence must allow traversal through:

```text
SOURCE
→ ACQUISITION
→ INTERPRETATION
→ NORMALIZATION
→ VALIDATION
→ ELIGIBILITY
→ MATERIALIZATION
→ DATASET
→ MANIFEST
→ PUBLICATION
→ CONSUMER
```

Applicable OBS evidence must establish:

```text
FAULT
→ DETECTION
→ ATTRIBUTED SIGNAL
→ RESTRICTIVE RESPONSE
→ CONTAINMENT
```

Timing evidence includes occurrence, detection, restrictive-response, and containment times only where material. No acceptable latency is inferred without an approved policy.

Applicable SEC evidence covers Stage-3 integrity, including:

- source and artifact identity;
- schema, policy, and version integrity;
- quarantine-release integrity;
- provenance and historical-record integrity;
- manifest/content binding;
- publication integrity;
- hosted candidate identity.

Stage-9 enterprise security remains outside Stage 3.

## 11. RECOVERY / REPRODUCIBILITY EVIDENCE

This class is applicable to claims involving:

- historical reconstruction;
- deterministic rematerialization;
- exact-version retrieval;
- interrupted ingestion or materialization;
- atomic publication;
- invalidation propagation and recovery;
- immutable historical evidence;
- correction-aware reconstruction;
- persistence integrity.

Required proof distinguishes:

- restart from actual recovery;
- recovered data from reauthorized data;
- preserved history from current eligibility;
- deterministic reconstruction from merely successful execution.

Property-specific `NOT_APPLICABLE_WITH_RATIONALE` remains permitted. Blanket N/A is prohibited.

## 12. INDEPENDENCE / HOSTED EVIDENCE

Independent evidence must identify:

- evaluator;
- evidence producer;
- evaluated subject;
- evaluation workflow or source;
- independence relationship;
- evaluator version where material;
- result and limitations;
- exact candidate and artifact identities.

Hosted/trusted evidence must preserve:

- repository;
- commit and tree;
- base and head;
- PR identity where applicable;
- workflow and trusted reference;
- event;
- run, job, and attempt;
- artifact name, ID, and digest;
- conclusion;
- relevant protection context.

A successful run against another commit, tree, base, environment, or artifact cannot satisfy the candidate’s obligation.

## 13. CURRENTNESS / INVALIDATION MODEL

Evidence lifecycle transitions are dependency-driven:

```text
CURRENT → STALE
CURRENT → INVALIDATED
CURRENT → SUPERSEDED
UNKNOWN → CURRENT only after attributable establishment
UNKNOWN → remains UNKNOWN while unresolved
STALE → CURRENT only after required review/revalidation
```

Triggers include:

- implementation or identity change;
- requirement, control, test, or scenario change;
- provider/source semantic change;
- schema or interpretation change;
- calendar/timezone change;
- policy or dependency change;
- material environment change;
- contradictory incident;
- review horizon reached;
- artifact loss or unavailability;
- retention event affecting reproducibility.

The required process is:

```text
CHANGE
→ DEPENDENCY GRAPH
→ AFFECTED-SET CALCULATION
→ EVIDENCE STATE TRANSITION
→ GATE REASSESSMENT
```

Unknown blast radius produces `AFFECTED_SCOPE = UNKNOWN`.

## 14. RETENTION MODEL

Physical retention and evidence identity retention remain distinct.

Where protected evidence refers to a physically removed or archived object, retain:

- stable object identity;
- attributable content/version identity;
- provenance relationship;
- disposition;
- retention-policy identity;
- when and why it became unavailable;
- dependent evidence relationships.

If required source material becomes unavailable:

- reproducibility must be reassessed;
- limitations become explicit;
- dependent evidence may become stale, invalidated, or unknown;
- no claim may pretend reproducibility is unchanged.

No retention duration or storage technology is selected.

## 15. EVIDENCE PACKAGE MODEL

| Package | Contents |
|---|---|
| A. Requirement/Behavior | Requirement clauses, implementation identity, direct behavioral results |
| B. Adversarial | Scenario execution, restrictive response, forbidden-outcome proof |
| C. Control Effectiveness | Control challenge and observed effectiveness |
| D. Provenance | Source, temporal roles, transformations, dependencies, identities |
| E. Recovery/Reproducibility | Reconstruction, interruption, recovery, exact retrieval |
| F. Hosted/Trusted Candidate | Exact candidate workflows, jobs, artifacts, digests |
| G. Independent Review | Evaluator separation, reviewed subject, result, limitations |
| H. Gate Candidate | Complete pre-closure evidence index and assessment |
| I. Closure/Protection | Merge, protected state, post-merge runs and protection evidence |

Shared evidence is referenced through stable identities. It is not copied into multiple packages as separate apparent proof.

## 16. GATE-S03-01 PACKAGE

The gate package must contain:

- exact candidate commit/tree;
- all locked applicable requirements and applicability dispositions;
- implementation identities;
- direct clause evidence;
- adversarial evidence;
- control-effectiveness evidence;
- applicable recovery/reproducibility evidence;
- provenance and invalidation evidence;
- assumptions/reality gaps and their consequences;
- resolved required decisions;
- blocker status;
- independent-review evidence;
- natural hosted/trusted candidate checks;
- completeness manifest;
- contradiction assessment;
- explicit authority ceiling.

It must not include nonexistent future merge or post-merge evidence.

## 17. POST-MERGE CLOSURE PACKAGE

The separate closure/protection package must establish:

- owner merge authorization;
- merge method and identity;
- protected tree and bytes;
- ordered topology where applicable;
- candidate-to-protected reconciliation;
- natural post-merge workflow;
- post-merge job and artifact identities;
- post-merge conclusions;
- protection and trusted-control state;
- absence of unexpected changes;
- final lifecycle determination.

A historical gate PASS remains attributable if closure fails, but Stage 3 cannot be declared closed/protected.

## 18. COMPLETENESS MANIFEST

The logical manifest indexes:

- exact candidate;
- applicable obligation identities;
- satisfying evidence identities;
- evidence states;
- sufficiency assessments;
- dependency currentness;
- independence status;
- unresolved obligations;
- contradictions;
- gate consumers.

Required controls:

- all references resolve;
